3 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284333 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284333 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e275f989-8b1b-539a-87a4-fba67d62a361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284336 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e3ab9ad5-298c-5e24-9d44-dc14f057d818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+14{x}_{3}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bda4f08-ef7b-5966-96c3-3621b7deb316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284632 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "315ff370-acb2-54c0-a94c-7932c53e50d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284645 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-12-\\lambda  & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-256 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -20)(\\lambda +20) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =20,\\,-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388518 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186388522 #edb/ref 175921860454