ef 17592186045417] [17592186389643 #edb/ref 1071 "The minimum value of $Q(x)=8{x}_{1}^{2}-30{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-50{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $4{x}_{1}^{2}+25{x}_{2}^{2}=100,$ is $-250.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "546ae5ae-d06c-5386-b960-6a8c9d016f97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389643 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=5$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{5}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -75 \\\\ -30 & -100\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-250,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{min}=-250.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-250:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-250I) & =[\\begin{aligned}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{aligned}]+250[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}450 & -150 \\\\ -150 & 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-250I))y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389644 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "807b9eea-f22c-56a9-981a-517ef2430f4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389645 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389645 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389645 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e97a677c-7cd3-5ab4-a853-3e58af95ef68" #edb